' ============================================================================
' 42 The elaboration of the positional operators: broadcasting and @.
'
' Source of Figure TL7 in ../tensor-logic/static-semantics.md (section 4).
' The figure restates the broadcasting rules of numpy and the cases of
' numpy.matmul that sections 4.1 and 4.2 of that document state. It adds no
' new fact.
'
' Reading order:
'   1. An elementwise operator aligns the two shapes from the right, pads the
'      shorter one with ones, and compares each axis.
'   2. The matrix product @ first handles the one-dimensional cases, then
'      multiplies the last two axes and broadcasts the leading axes.
'   3. Both branches give an index-form term with a fresh index for each
'      result axis, or exit with a typed code that names the shapes.
'
' Colors come from concept-colors.iuml (aliases of the shared palette).
' Render: ./render.sh 42-tl-broadcast-matmul.puml   Check: ./render.sh --check

@startuml 42-tl-broadcast-matmul
!include ../../../casper/theory/diagrams/palette.iuml
!include concept-colors.iuml
$ca_base_skin()
skinparam ArrowColor $rrs_relation
skinparam ActivityBorderColor $rrs_relation
skinparam ActivityDiamondBorderColor $rrs_relation
skinparam ActivityDiamondBackgroundColor $ca_note_amber
skinparam PartitionBorderColor $rrs_relation
skinparam NoteBorderColor $rrs_relation
skinparam LegendBorderColor $rrs_relation
skinparam LegendBackgroundColor $ca_note_grey
title The elaboration of the positional operators (numpy rules)

start
:a positional operator with operand
shapes a and b (ranks from the signature); <<$rrs_tl_term>>
if (the operator is @?) then (no: an elementwise operator)
  partition "Broadcasting (static semantics 4.1)" {
    :align the shapes from the right, and pad
    the shorter shape on the left with ones; <<$rrs_tl_engine>>
    while (an axis k is left?) is (yes)
      if (<latex>a_k = b_k</latex>, or <latex>a_k = 1</latex>, or <latex>b_k = 1</latex>?) then (yes)
        :the result size is <latex>\max(a_k, b_k)</latex>,
        and a size 0 against 1 gives 0; <<$rrs_tl_engine>>
      else (no)
        :BroadcastMismatch, naming both
        shapes and the negative axis; <<$rrs_reject>>
        stop
      endif
    endwhile (no)
    :a fresh index for each result axis
    an operand axis of size 1 reads its only element; <<$rrs_tl_engine>>
  }
else (yes)
  partition "Matrix product (static semantics 4.2)" {
    if (an operand is a scalar?) then (yes)
      :MatmulMismatch; <<$rrs_reject>>
      stop
    else (no)
    endif
    if (both operands are 1-D?) then (yes)
      :the inner product <latex>\sum_{j} A[j]\, B[j]</latex>; <<$rrs_tl_engine>>
    else (no)
      :a 1-D first operand gets a leading axis of size 1,
      which the result drops again
      a 1-D second operand gets a trailing axis of size 1; <<$rrs_tl_engine>>
      if (the inner sizes of the last two axes match?) then (yes)
        :the last two axes multiply:
        <latex>C[\ldots, i, l] = \sum_{j} A[\ldots, i, j]\, B[\ldots, j, l]</latex>
        the leading axes broadcast as above; <<$rrs_tl_engine>>
      else (no)
        :MatmulMismatch, naming the inner sizes; <<$rrs_reject>>
        stop
      endif
    endif
  }
endif
:an index-form term of the core,
with an explicit sum over the inner axis; <<$rrs_tl_term>>
stop

legend bottom
**Concept colors**
|<$rrs_tl_term>   | a TL term: the sugar in, the core out |
|<$rrs_tl_engine> | a step of ELABORATE |
|<$rrs_reject>    | a typed failure |
endlegend
@enduml
